"Ofer Inbar" <cos@polyamory.org> wrote:

> [visible spell-knowledge-time-left counter]

That would be probably fairly fair except, as you
say, for spoiling the unspoiled player, but you can
(fairly) easily just name each spellbook with the
turn at which it will expire, as you read it, and
use that as notes.  I do that in some of my more
obsessive games.

> [helm of brilliance should extend spell memory]

This one I really like, and the implementation
wouldn't be as hard as you suggest. Instead of
changing every spell counter each time you put on or
take off the helm, just use my comment in another
thread, and make the counter decrement code check
for a helm, and if worn, decrement the code
probabilistically instead of absolutely, so that if
your helm enchantment rated say a change from a
20000 turn forget period to a 30000 turn forget
period, the code would still start the counter at
20000, but decrement the spell counter with a 2/3rds
probability at each turn you wore the helm, 3/3rds
probability each turn you did not wear the helm.

That trick is (or used to be) used lots of places in
NetHack to get fractional behavior out of integer
quantities.

